"""Triton implementation of ``aten::_foreach_tanh_`` (in-place, Tensor[]).

Applies ``tanh`` independently to every tensor of the list, writing the result
back into the same storage (storage pointer, offset, shape and stride are all
preserved).  The call returns ``None``.

Architecture
------------
* one launch per ``(device, dtype)`` group instead of one per tensor: up to 16
  flat-addressable tensors share a single kernel, selected by a compile-time
  unrolled branch on ``program_id(0)``.  Every pointer/length travels as an
  ordinary kernel argument, so no per-call host metadata buffer (and no
  host-to-device copy) is needed;
* when every tensor of the launch has the same element count the group kernel
  takes a single length argument, and when that count is also a multiple of the
  block there is no length argument and no mask at all: predication costs a
  vector compare plus a fill of the masked lanes on every tile, which is pure
  overhead on the fully covered tiles that make up the bulk of a large tensor;
* contiguous or dense/flat-addressable tensors are traversed flat (the memory
  span is exactly the tensor's element set, which is all an element-wise op
  needs); genuinely gappy views use a rank-specialized stride-decoding kernel;
* one tile per program: a multi-tile ``tl.range`` variant measured ~2x slower
  on this backend, so the single-tile form is deliberate;
* block sizes are workload-derived classes, never physical-core constants.

Only floating point inputs are accepted; integer/bool get the ``RuntimeError``
ATen raises for an in-place transcendental, stride-0 writes raise the ATen
overlap error, and an empty list raises.
"""

